window.__labLoaded({"id":"2.2","title":"Counting: How Many Ways?","goal":"Count podiums, committees and lottery tickets with factorials, permutations and combinations, and confirm the counts by listing the possibilities.","question":"A school chess club has 9 members and a new coach. The coach asks: in how many ways can we pick a captain, a vice-captain and a treasurer, and in how many ways can we pick a 3-person team to travel to a match? And a different question from a lottery fan: what is the chance of winning if you pick 6 numbers out of 49? Can counting rules answer all of these without listing everything?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Factorials","text":"n! is the number of ways to line up n different things. Python's math module has factorial built in. By convention 0! equals 1.","code":"import math\n\nfor n in [0, 1, 4, 5, 8]:\n    print(f'{n}! = {math.factorial(n)}')","explain":["Load Python's math module, which has counting functions built in.","Loop over a few values of n to see how fast factorials grow.","Print n! for each one. The factorial n! is the number of ways to line up n different things."],"meaning":"0! = 1 (by convention, one way to arrange nothing), 4! = 24, 5! = 120 and 8! = 40320. Factorials grow very fast, which is why counting by hand quickly becomes impossible and we need formulas.","out":"0! = 1\n1! = 1\n4! = 24\n5! = 120\n8! = 40320","figs":[]},{"title":"Permutations and combinations","text":"math.perm(n, r) counts ordered selections (a podium of 3 from 8 runners). math.comb(n, r) counts unordered ones (a 3-person committee from 10). Dividing the permutations by r! gives the combinations.","code":"print('podiums, perm(8, 3)       =', math.perm(8, 3))\nprint('committees, comb(10, 3)   =', math.comb(10, 3))\nprint('perm(10, 3) / 3!          =', math.perm(10, 3) // math.factorial(3))\nprint('poker hands, comb(52, 5)  =', math.comb(52, 5))","explain":["Count ordered selections: a podium of 3 from 8 runners. perm(n, r) is used when the order of the picks matters.","Count unordered selections: a 3-person committee from 10. comb(n, r) is used when only who is picked matters, not the order.","Divide the permutations by 3! (the ways to reorder 3 people). This shows the link between the two counts.","Count 5-card poker hands. A hand is a group, so order does not matter."],"meaning":"There are 336 podiums but only 120 committees. The third line shows why: perm(10, 3) / 3! is also 120, so each committee of 3 appears in 6 different orders. A poker hand can be chosen in 2,598,960 ways.","out":"podiums, perm(8, 3)       = 336\ncommittees, comb(10, 3)   = 120\nperm(10, 3) / 3!          = 120\npoker hands, comb(52, 5)  = 2598960","figs":[]},{"title":"List them to confirm","text":"For small cases we can write out every selection with itertools and count it. Choosing 3 of 5 people gives 60 ordered lists but only 10 groups, a ratio of 3! = 6.","code":"from itertools import permutations, combinations\n\npeople = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E']\nordered = list(permutations(people, 3))\ngroups = list(combinations(people, 3))\n\nprint('ordered lists:', len(ordered), ' formula:', math.perm(5, 3))\nprint('groups:', len(groups), ' formula:', math.comb(5, 3))\nprint('first groups:', [''.join(g) for g in groups[:4]])","explain":["Load permutations and combinations, which list the actual selections and not just count them.","Name five people so we can list real selections.","List every ordered way to pick 3 of the 5. Each item is a different line-up.","List every group of 3 of the 5, ignoring order.","Compare the number of ordered lists with the formula. They must agree.","Compare the number of groups with the formula, and show the groups agree too.","Show the first four groups to see what a combination looks like."],"meaning":"Listing gives 60 ordered lists and 10 groups, the same as the formulas perm(5, 3) = 60 and comb(5, 3) = 10. The ratio is 6 = 3!. Checking a formula against a small list is how you gain trust in it before using it on big numbers.","out":"ordered lists: 60  formula: 60\ngroups: 10  formula: 10\nfirst groups: ['ABC', 'ABD', 'ABE', 'ACD']","figs":[]},{"title":"From counting to probability","text":"One lottery ticket wins out of every possible set of 6 numbers from 49. The chance is 1 divided by the number of tickets. The all-hearts hand is (ways to pick 5 hearts) divided by (all hands).","code":"tickets = math.comb(49, 6)\nprint(f'tickets = {tickets:,}')\nprint(f'P(jackpot) = {1 / tickets:.2e}')\n\nhearts = math.comb(13, 5) / math.comb(52, 5)\nprint(f'P(5 hearts) = {math.comb(13, 5)} / {math.comb(52, 5):,} = {hearts:.4f}')","explain":["Count all possible lottery tickets: ways to choose 6 numbers from 49.","Print the count with thousands separators.","One ticket wins, so the chance is 1 divided by the number of tickets. The e notation means a very small number.","Count the hands made of 5 hearts and divide by all hands to get the probability.","Print the counts and the probability so every piece is visible."],"meaning":"There are 13,983,816 tickets, so the jackpot chance is 7.15e-08 (about 1 in 14 million). The chance of a 5-card hand with all hearts is 1287 / 2,598,960 = 0.0005. Both are favourable counts divided by all counts, which is the basic probability rule.","out":"tickets = 13,983,816\nP(jackpot) = 7.15e-08\nP(5 hearts) = 1287 / 2,598,960 = 0.0005","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"factorial(4)                 # 4!\nchoose(10, 3)                # committees\nchoose(8, 3) * factorial(3)  # podiums (permutations)\nchoose(52, 5)                # poker hands\nchoose(49, 6)                # lottery tickets\n1 / choose(49, 6)\nncol(combn(5, 3))            # lists the 10 groups\nchoose(13, 5) / choose(52, 5)","explain":["Factorial of 4, which is the number of ways to line up 4 things.","choose(n, k) counts groups: committees of 3 from 10.","R has no permutation function, so multiply choose(8, 3) by 3! to count ordered podiums.","Count 5-card poker hands.","Count lottery tickets of 6 from 49.","One ticket wins, so the chance is 1 over the count.","combn(5, 3) lists all groups of 3 from 5; ncol counts them (10).","Hearts-only hands divided by all hands."],"expect":"24; 120; 336; 2598960; 13983816; 7.151e-08; 10; 0.0005","read":"Read the numbers in order: 24, 120, 336, 2598960, 13983816, then 7.151e-08, 10 and about 0.0005. They match Python. The 336 podiums are more than the 120 committees because order matters for podiums.","note":"Base R has choose() and factorial() but no permutation function, so we multiply choose(n, r) by factorial(r). Python's value for 5 hearts is 0.0005 (0.000495)."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use the Arithmetic functions, LNGAMMA is in the list)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE fact4 = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(5))).\nCOMPUTE perm83 = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(9) - LNGAMMA(6))).\nCOMPUTE comb103 = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(11) - LNGAMMA(4) - LNGAMMA(8))).\nCOMPUTE lottery = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(50) - LNGAMMA(7) - LNGAMMA(44))).\nFORMATS fact4 TO lottery (F10.0).\nLIST VARIABLES=fact4 perm83 comb103 lottery.","explain":["Make a tiny data set so there is one row to hold our answers.","Start the data block.","One row containing the value 1.","End the data block.","Compute 4! using LNGAMMA: LNGAMMA(5) is the log of 4!, EXP undoes the log, RND rounds away tiny errors.","Permutations of 3 from 8 are 8!/5!, which is a difference of log-factorials.","Combinations of 3 from 10 are 10!/(3! 7!), done with logs.","Lottery tickets: 49!/(6! 43!), done with logs.","Show the results as whole numbers with no decimals.","Print the four answers."],"read":"In the LIST output look at the four columns: fact4 is 24, perm83 is 336, comb103 is 120 and lottery is 13983816. These match Python. SPSS has no factorial menu, so we use the log-gamma trick.","note":"SPSS has no built-in factorial or combination menu item. The closest honest route is LNGAMMA, because LNGAMMA(n + 1) is the log of n!. We turn it back with EXP and round, which gives 24, 336, 120 and 13983816."},"stata":{"code":"display factorial(4)\ndisplay comb(10, 3)\ndisplay comb(8, 3) * factorial(3)\ndisplay comb(52, 5)\ndisplay %12.0f comb(49, 6)\ndisplay 1/comb(49, 6)\ndisplay comb(13, 5)/comb(52, 5)","explain":["Stata has factorial(): 4! is 24.","comb(n, k) counts groups: 3 of 10 is 120.","Stata has no permutation function, so multiply comb(8, 3) by 3! for podiums.","Poker hands: 5 of 52.","Lottery tickets with %12.0f so the big number is not shown in scientific notation.","One ticket in 13.98 million wins.","Probability of an all-heart hand."],"read":"The seven lines print 24, 120, 336, 2598960, 13983816, about 7.15e-08 and about .000495. They agree with Python (0.0005 is that last value rounded). Each display line prints its own answer.","note":"Stata's comb(n, k) and factorial() match Python; there is no permutation function, so podiums are comb(8, 3) times 3!. The %12.0f format stops the big lottery number from showing in scientific notation."},"conclusion":"Picking a captain, vice-captain and treasurer from 9 members can be done in 504 ways because order matters, and picking a 3-person team in 84 ways because it does not. The lottery jackpot has a 1 in 13,983,816 chance. The caution: the hard part is deciding whether order matters, since the wrong choice multiplies the count by 3! or r!.","practice":{"scenario":"A robotics club has 9 members. The coach must name a captain, a vice-captain and a treasurer (different people, order matters). Separately, 3 members will be chosen as a delegation to a competition. A raffle also draws 5 winning numbers from 30.","data":"Members: 9. Offices: 3 distinct roles. Delegation: 3 people. Raffle: choose 5 numbers from 30.","tasks":["How many ways are there to fill the three offices, and how many delegations of 3 are there?","Divide the first count by the second. What does the ratio mean?","What is the chance of winning the raffle with one ticket?","Ana and Ben are two of the 9. What is the chance both are in the delegation?"],"hint":"Offices have an order, so use math.perm; a delegation has none, so use math.comb. For Ana and Ben, count the delegations that already contain both: only the third member is free.","solution":{"code":"import math\n\ncaptain_vice_treasurer = math.perm(9, 3)\ndelegation = math.comb(9, 3)\nprint('officer line-ups:', captain_vice_treasurer)\nprint('delegations     :', delegation)\nprint('ratio           :', captain_vice_treasurer // delegation, '= 3!')\n\ndraw = math.comb(30, 5)\nprint(f'raffle draws: {draw:,}, P(win) = {1 / draw:.2e}')\nprint(f'P(Ana and Ben both on the delegation) = {math.comb(7, 1)}/{delegation} = {math.comb(7, 1) / delegation:.4f}')","answer":"There are perm(9, 3) = 504 ways to fill the offices and comb(9, 3) = 84 delegations. The ratio is 6, which is 3!, the number of ways to reorder the same 3 people. The raffle has 142,506 draws, so one ticket wins with probability 7.02e-06. With Ana and Ben fixed, 7 delegations remain, so the chance is 7/84 = 0.0833.","out":"officer line-ups: 504\ndelegations     : 84\nratio           : 6 = 3!\nraffle draws: 142,506, P(win) = 7.02e-06\nP(Ana and Ben both on the delegation) = 7/84 = 0.0833","figs":[]}}});
